  {
    "title": "The Rohingya Language: Origins, Development, and Identity",
    "icon": "history_edu",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "    The Rohingya language is an Eastern Indo-Aryan language spoken by the Rohingya people of the Arakan (Rakhine) coastal region of Myanmar and throughout a widespread global diaspora. With roots extending back more than two thousand years, the language reflects a layered history of migration, cultural contact, scholarship, and survival. Its modern form carries within it echoes of ancient Indo-Aryan dialects, medieval Islamic influence, and colonial-era transformations while maintaining its own distinctive phonology, grammar, and vocabulary."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "    This chapter provides a comprehensive overview of the Rohingya language: its prehistoric origins, its development across successive historical periods, its interactions with neighboring cultures, and the modern effort to preserve and standardize it through the Hanifi Rohingya script."
      },
      {
        "type": "timeline",
        "events": [
          {
            "title": "ANCIENT FOUNDATIONS (1st Millennium BCE – Early Centuries CE)",
            "description": "The earliest roots of the Rohingya language lie in the spread of Eastern Indo-Aryan speech varieties. These dialects descended from Old Indo-Aryan (Vedic Sanskrit) and evolved independently along the eastern Gangetic and coastal Bengal regions. Over centuries, they transformed into Middle Indo-Aryan forms known as Prakrits. Early dialects emerged along the coastal belt of the Bay of Bengal, encountering Tibeto-Burman and Austroasiatic languages."
          },
          {
            "title": "EARLY MEDIEVAL PERIOD (5th–10th Century)",
            "description": "Eastern Prakrits evolved into Apabhraṃśa and then into proto-regional languages. Coastal Arakan dialects diverged from Chittagonian and proto-Bengali, forming a unique linguistic identity influenced by the local population and geography."
          },
          {
            "title": "ISLAMIC AND PERSIANATE INFLUENCE (10th–15th Century)",
            "description": "Muslim merchants and Sufi travelers introduced Arabic and Persian vocabulary. Arabic contributed religious terms, while Persian contributed administrative and literary terminology. These influences created a cultural and lexical layer within Rohingya."
          },
          {
            "title": "THE MRAUK-U COSMOPOLITAN ERA (15th–18th Century)",
            "description": "During the Mrauk-U Kingdom, Arakan became a regional cultural hub. The court employed Persian and Bengali Muslim scholars, poets, and administrators. Rohingya absorbed Persian, Arabic, and Bengali vocabulary, strengthening its linguistic cohesion and cultural identity."
          },
          {
            "title": "BURMESE AND BRITISH RULE (1784–1948)",
            "description": "Burmese conquest introduced new vocabulary, while British colonial rule introduced English terms and literacy practices. Rohingya was written in multiple scripts: Perso-Arabic, Burmese, and Roman. Fragmented writing traditions slowed literacy but increased adaptability."
          },
          {
            "title": "MODERN ERA AND THE HANIFI SCRIPT (20th Century – Present)",
            "description": "In the 1980s, Maulana Mohammad Hanif and scholars created the Hanifi Rohingya script to represent the language’s phonology accurately. Unicode inclusion in 2019 enabled digital literacy. Today, Rohingya is revitalized globally through schools, digital platforms, and cultural preservation."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "info_card",
        "title": "LINGUISTIC PROFILE",
        "content": "Rohingya retains core Eastern Indo-Aryan grammar with rich vowel contrasts, aspiration, nasalization, and verb morphology. Vocabulary layers include Indo-Aryan, Arabic, Persian, Urdu, Bengali, Burmese, and English influences."
      },
      {
        "type": "info_card",
        "title": "TIMELINE SUMMARY",
        "content": "• 1000–300 BCE: Eastern Indo-Aryan dialects reach Bengal–Arakan coast.\n• 300 BCE–200 CE: Eastern Prakrits develop; early Arakan dialects appear.\n• 200–800 CE: Proto-Rohingya forms from Apabhraṃśa-derived vernaculars.\n• 900–1400 CE: Arabic and Persian influence begins.\n• 1430–1784 CE: Mrauk-U Era: Persianate and Bengali Muslim integration.\n• 1784–1948: Burmese and British rule introduce new vocabulary and literacy forms.\n• 1980s: Hanifi Rohingya script created.\n• 2019: Hanifi Rohingya enters Unicode.\n• Present: Global revitalization, digital literacy, cultural preservation."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Moulana Mufti Muhammad Hanif Ragheb",
    "icon": "person",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "image",
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/arakaneserohingya/LearnRhgAssets/main/MolanaHanifRageb.png"
      },
      {
        "type": "info_card",
        "title": "Early Life & Family",
        "content": "    Moulana Sheikh Allama Muhammad Hanif Ragheb Arkani (may Allah have mercy on him) was born in 1952 CE (1371 AH / 1164 Rohingya) in the village of Firing Daung, northwest of Rathidaung Township, Arakan (Rakhine State). He was the son of Wali Ahmed, grandson of Turab Ali, and great-grandson of Nurud Din. Allah blessed him with nine children: three sons—Professor Hassan Hanif, Professor Emran Hanif, and Professor Ebrahim Hanif—and six daughters. From his early childhood, he showed a natural love for knowledge and a deep sense of piety."
      },
      {
        "type": "info_card",
        "title": "Educational Journey",
        "content": "    His journey of learning began at Qasim al-Ulum Friending School. Later, he studied at Rahmaniyah School under Sheikh Din Muhammad (the martyr) and Sheikh Maulana Abdul Haq, gaining strong knowledge in Arabic grammar. He pursued higher studies at Darul Uloom Miyajanpur, Moin al-Islam Hathazari, and Zamiriyah Qasim al-Ulum Youth University, completing Daurah Hadith in 1977. He qualified as a Mufti in 1981 at Dar al-Iftaa, Karachi. In later years, he was honored to memorize the entire Holy Qur’an with tajweed."
      },
      {
        "type": "info_card",
        "title": "Father of the Rohingya Script",
        "content": "    Sheikh Muhammad Hanif was a reformer. During his student life, he worked to codify the Rohingya script. In 1982, he created a new writing system with distinct alphabets to preserve the Rohingya language and culture. Today, the Rohingya community proudly has its own written language, taught in schools and learning centers, keeping his vision alive."
      },
      {
        "type": "info_card",
        "title": "Contributions & Authorship",
        "content": "    He taught in Maungdaw, Akyab, Karachi, and Cox’s Bazar, founding Dar al-Tarbiyah Islamic School (1985) and the Islamic Postgraduate Studies and Research Complex (2002). He authored valuable books in Arabic, Persian, and Urdu, including 'Quiliyat Ragheb', 'Izhar-e-Haqiqat', 'The Call of Truth', and 'The Problem of Arakan and Its Solution'."
      },
      {
        "type": "info_card",
        "title": "Legacy & Character",
        "content": "    Admired for his humility, wisdom, and eloquence, he possessed deep knowledge of Islamic jurisprudence, literature, and poetry. He devoted his life to teaching, issuing fatwas, and guiding the Rohingya people with sincerity."
      },
      {
        "type": "info_card",
        "title": "Passing",
        "content": "    Sheikh Muhammad Hanif Ragheb Arkani passed away on Friday, 10 November 2023 (27 Khati Mash 1235 Rohingya) at Dar al-Sunnah University, Cox’s Bazar. His funeral was attended by thousands. He was buried in Safrang, Nowa Farah. His legacy inspires the Rohingya people and the Muslim Ummah. May Allah grant him the highest place in Jannah."
      }
    ]
